\section{Error Analysis \emph{[Exploratory]}}
\label{sec:analysis}

The following failure mode taxonomy emerges empirically from LLM-coded traces, distinct from the input ambiguity taxonomy (\S\ref{sec:taxonomy}).

We analyze 614 violation traces across all five models, coded into five failure modes: unauthorized escalation, assumption-based action, scope misapplication, arbitrary rule selection, and conservative refusal. GPT-5.4 and GPT-4.1 serve as detailed annotation anchors because they span the judge boundary. Inter-coder reliability is substantial (Gwet's AC$_1$ $= 0.62$ \citep{gwet_2014}; Cohen's $\kappa = 0.52$ \citep{cohen_1960}; the gap reflects the prevalence paradox, as three categories account for ${\sim}$90\% of instances).

\subsection{Type-Specific Failure Mechanisms}

The five-model data reveal three mechanistically distinct failure patterns that crosscut the specification/linguistic layer boundary.

\emph{Structural misparse} (scope misapplication): the agent constructs an incorrect parse and follows it consistently, paralleling garden-path processing \citep{frazier_rayner_1982,ferreira_good_enough_2002}---e.g., parsing ``items purchased with a coupon or during a sale are final'' as restricting only coupon purchases. This dominates scopal (all 5 models), conditional precedence (all 5), and coreferential (3/5).

\emph{Information gap-filling} (assumption-based action): the agent fills missing information with plausible but incorrect defaults---e.g., inventing a 30-day return window when none is specified. This dominates incompleteness (3/5) and lexical (3/5).

\emph{Boundary violation} (unauthorized escalation): the agent exceeds permission boundaries when authorization scope is underspecified---e.g., issuing a refund above its stated limit rather than escalating; 69.2\% of such violations involve unauthorized escalation with zero conservative refusals ($p < 10^{-4}$, binomial test), revealing systematic permissiveness bias. This dominates authorization scope exclusively (all 5 models).

Each type's dominant failure mode is majority-consistent ($\geq$3/5 models agree), with three types achieving full unanimity. The layer distinction does not hold at the mechanistic level: conditional precedence (specification-layer) shares its dominant mode with scopal (linguistic-layer), while authorization scope follows a boundary-violation pattern absent from other specification-layer types.

\subsection{Default Single-Parse Adoption}

Agents rarely recognize ambiguity. We code \emph{disambiguation awareness} (acknowledging multiple interpretations, asking clarifying questions, or conditioning on ambiguity) versus \emph{surface adoption} (proceeding with a single interpretation). Overall disambiguation awareness is 4.6\%: Claude (11.0\%) and GPT-4.1 (10.7\%) show the highest rates, while Qwen3 and DeepSeek show zero across all 278 combined violation traces.

We term this \emph{Default Single-Parse Adoption}: unlike humans---who typically signal uncertainty or seek clarification---agents proceed with full confidence in their chosen interpretation, consistent with \citet{hu_ambiguity_2025}'s finding that ambiguity in LLMs reflects missing conceptual representations.
\emph{Default} is critical: agents are not inherently incapable of disambiguation---our mitigation experiment shows $-$8.5\,pp improvement when prompted---but default to single-parse adoption without explicit instruction, paralleling human good-enough processing \citep{ferreira_misinterpretation_2002}.

\subsection{Systematic Nocuity}

Among ambiguous clauses, 41.3\% trigger majority-convergent violations ($\geq$3/5 models), with 13\% producing universal violations. This extends \citet{berry_kamsties_2004}'s framework with \emph{systematic nocuity}: all models converge on the same incorrect interpretation, invisible in agreement-based evaluation. Incompleteness has the highest universal convergence (24\%).

